No, there are many more than three. While XSS (Client-Side), SQL Injection (Database), and RCE (Server-Side) are three of the most famous because they cover the three main layers of a web application, they represent only a fraction of the threat landscape. [1] 
Security frameworks like the OWASP Top 10 categorize dozens of different attack types that target completely different vulnerabilities in application logic, business workflows, and network infrastructure. [2, 3] 
------------------------------
## 🗺️ The Broader Threat Landscape (Beyond the Main 3)
To give you an universal view of bug hunting, vulnerabilities are generally broken down into several major categories depending on what part of the application they target: [4, 5] 
## 1. Broken Access Control & Logic Flaws
These attacks happen when an application correctly handles inputs but fails to verify who is allowed to see the data. [6, 7, 8] 

* IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference): Changing an ID parameter in a URL (e.g., from ?user_id=1001 to ?user_id=1002) to view another user's private account or data without permission.
* Privilege Escalation: Exploiting a flaw to turn a regular customer account into an administrator account. [9, 10, 11, 12, 13] 

## 2. Infrastructure & Integration Flaws
These attacks target how a server talks to other computers, internal APIs, or file networks.

* SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery): Forcing the vulnerable server to act as a proxy and make unauthorized internal network requests to private backend systems that are normally hidden from the public internet. [14, 15, 16, 17] 
* XXE (XML External Entity): Injecting malicious references into an XML data parser to force the server to read local files or interact with internal networks. [18, 19, 20, 21, 22] 

## 3. Cryptographic & Authentication Failures
These flaws target the mechanisms that handle user identities, passwords, and sessions. [23, 24, 25, 26, 27] 

* Session Fixation / Hijacking: Exploiting weak token-generation patterns to predict or steal active user session keys.
* Brute Forcing & Credential Stuffing: Automated guessing of passwords or spraying leaked credentials across login interfaces that lack rate-limiting shields. [28, 29, 30, 31, 32] 

## 4. Supply Chain & Configuration Vulnerabilities
These risks exist within the building blocks and settings of the system.

* Security Misconfigurations: Leaving default administrative passwords active, keeping debugging screens turned on in production, or exposing internal folder structures. [33, 34, 35] 
* Vulnerable Dependencies: Using outdated open-source library packages (like old npm or pip packages) that contain pre-known public vulnerabilities. [36, 37, 38] 

